. *****************************************************************************
> ***
. *** Models:
. 
. * IMPORTANT NOTE: 
. * We specified in our PAP that we use a fixed effects regression model with s
> tandard errors clustered on the household level. We furthermore specified in 
> the Note under formula 2 that we use the Stata command "xtreg, fe" to estimat
> e these models. But since individuals are in some cases cross-classified acro
> ss households due to leaving or joining a household, the "xtreg, fe" command 
> is not suitable to cluster the standard errors correspondingly as it expects 
> the clustering to be strictly hierachical. We therefore have to deviate from 
> our PAP in one or another way to achieve the stated goal to estimate fixed ef
> fects regression models with standard errors clustered on the household level
> :
. * 1) Our prefered option is to use the "reghdfe" command which also estimates
>  fixed effects regression models and yields exactly the same coefficients as 
> "xtreg, fe" but allows for clustering standard errors in the case of cross-cl
> assification.
. * 2) We force strictly hierarchical nesting on the household level by using t
> he first observed household identifier and then use the "xtreg, fe" command t
> o estimate the model.
. 
. * If you as the #Manydaughters project team do not have a priority regarding 
> this, we would prefer the model estimated with "reghdfe" as it is slightly mo
> re conservative than the "xtreg, fe" model.
. 
. *** Solution 1: (Our preferred solution since this can cluster standard error
> s at the current household level) 
. reghdfe `dep_var' fc##dvs2 `new_varlist', vce(cluster hid) abs(pid)
(MWFE estimator converged in 1 iterations)
